<ReportItem port="22" svc_name="ssh" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="187315" pluginName="SSH Terrapin Prefix Truncation Weakness (CVE-2023-48795)" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<cvss3_base_score>5.9</cvss3_base_score>
<cvss3_vector>CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N</cvss3_vector>
<cvss4_base_score>5.3</cvss4_base_score>
<cvss4_vector>CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:H/AT:N/PR:N/UI:N/VC:N/VI:H/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N</cvss4_vector>
<cvss_base_score>5.4</cvss_base_score>
<cvss_vector>CVSS2#AV:N/AC:H/Au:N/C:N/I:C/A:N</cvss_vector>
<cve>CVE-2023-48795</cve>
<description>The remote SSH server is vulnerable to a man-in-the-middle prefix truncation weakness known as Terrapin. An attacker on the network path can drop messages at the start of the secure channel without either side noticing, which can downgrade the security of the connection, for example by disabling keystroke timing obfuscation.

The weakness affects the chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com cipher and the encrypt-then-MAC (-etm@openssh.com) MAC algorithms when strict key exchange is not supported by both sides.

Note that Nessus has not tested for this issue but has instead relied only on the algorithms the server advertises.</description>
<plugin_name>SSH Terrapin Prefix Truncation Weakness (CVE-2023-48795)</plugin_name>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://terrapin-attack.com/
https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-48795</see_also>
<solution>Upgrade the SSH server to a release that supports strict key exchange (OpenSSH 9.6 or later), or disable the affected cipher and MAC algorithms.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote SSH server is vulnerable to a man-in-the-middle prefix truncation attack.</synopsis>
<plugin_output>
Supports following ChaCha20-Poly1305 Client to Server algorithm : chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com
Supports following ChaCha20-Poly1305 Server to Client algorithm : chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com
Supports following Encrypt-then-MAC Client to Server algorithms : umac-64-etm@openssh.com, umac-128-etm@openssh.com, hmac-sha2-256-etm@openssh.com
Supports following Encrypt-then-MAC Server to Client algorithms : umac-64-etm@openssh.com, umac-128-etm@openssh.com, hmac-sha2-256-etm@openssh.com
</plugin_output>
</ReportItem>
